1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,481
<font color="#FFFFFF">Classifier-free guidance, or CFG,</font>

2
00:00:04,561 --> 00:00:08,542
<font color="#FFFFFF">is the workhorse that lets conditional diffusion
models trade sample diversity against fidelity.</font>

3
00:00:08,622 --> 00:00:12,603
<font color="#FFFFFF">But when the condition is continuous, like a text
embedding,</font>

4
00:00:12,683 --> 00:00:16,664
<font color="#FFFFFF">CFG ignores that structure.</font>

5
00:00:16,744 --> 00:00:20,725
<font color="#FFFFFF">This ICLR 2024 paper from Tsinghua University and
Huawei introduces Inner Classifier-Free Guidance,</font>

6
00:00:20,805 --> 00:00:24,786
<font color="#FFFFFF">or ICFG,</font>

7
00:00:24,866 --> 00:00:28,847
<font color="#FFFFFF">a perspective that treats standard CFG as the
first-order case of a broader Taylor expansion.</font>

8
00:00:28,927 --> 00:00:32,908
<font color="#FFFFFF">By adding a second-order term, with no change to training,</font>

9
00:00:32,988 --> 00:00:36,969
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors get a better balance between fidelity
and diversity for Stable Diffusion,</font>

10
00:00:37,049 --> 00:00:41,030
<font color="#FFFFFF">using only a few lines of code.</font>

11
00:00:41,410 --> 00:00:48,851
<font color="#FFFFFF">Conditional diffusion models rely on
classifier-free guidance to control how diverse and faithful their samples are.</font>

12
00:00:48,931 --> 00:00:53,000
<font color="#FFFFFF">The problem is that CFG treats the condition as an
opaque label;</font>

13
00:00:53,080 --> 00:00:56,112
<font color="#FFFFFF">it places no constraints on the condition space.</font>

14
00:00:56,192 --> 00:01:00,196
<font color="#FFFFFF">So when that space is continuous, like a text
prompt embedding,</font>

15
00:01:00,276 --> 00:01:02,401
<font color="#FFFFFF">all that continuity goes to waste.</font>

16
00:01:02,481 --> 00:01:08,365
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors ask a pointed question: if the
condition lives in a structured continuous space,</font>

17
00:01:08,445 --> 00:01:10,440
<font color="#FFFFFF">can we do better than plain CFG?</font>

18
00:01:10,820 --> 00:01:17,203
<font color="#FFFFFF">There are two main ways to inject guidance into
diffusion models: use an external trained classifier,</font>

19
00:01:17,283 --> 00:01:21,490
<font color="#FFFFFF">or use classifier-free guidance that a single model
learns jointly.</font>

20
00:01:21,570 --> 00:01:26,673
<font color="#FFFFFF">Both work well, but neither says anything about the
shape of the condition space.</font>

21
00:01:26,753 --> 00:01:32,815
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors' insight is that a text encoder maps
prompts into a continuous space with structure,</font>

22
00:01:32,895 --> 00:01:36,527
<font color="#FFFFFF">and if you identify that structure, you can move along it.</font>

23
00:01:36,607 --> 00:01:39,470
<font color="#FFFFFF">That motivates inner classifier-free guidance.</font>

24
00:01:39,850 --> 00:01:44,761
<font color="#FFFFFF">The central contribution is a reframing: standard
CFG is not the whole story,</font>

25
00:01:44,841 --> 00:01:51,761
<font color="#FFFFFF">it is just the first-order term of a more general
expansion the authors call inner classifier-free guidance.</font>

26
00:01:51,841 --> 00:01:57,141
<font color="#FFFFFF">By writing the guided score as a Taylor series in
the guidance strength around one,</font>

27
00:01:57,221 --> 00:02:01,937
<font color="#FFFFFF">they recover CFG as the first-order case and then
add a second-order term.</font>

28
00:02:02,017 --> 00:02:07,446
<font color="#FFFFFF">This term is computed from the existing pretrained
model, so no retraining is needed.</font>

29
00:02:07,526 --> 00:02:11,400
<font color="#FFFFFF">They also provide a training policy, two sampling
algorithms,</font>

30
00:02:11,480 --> 00:02:13,150
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a convergence analysis.</font>

31
00:02:13,530 --> 00:02:14,933
<font color="#FFFFFF">Here is the mechanism.</font>

32
00:02:15,013 --> 00:02:20,662
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors write the guided distribution as the
unconditional distribution times the</font>

33
00:02:20,742 --> 00:02:23,223
<font color="#FFFFFF">ratio of conditional to unconditional,</font>

34
00:02:23,303 --> 00:02:26,796
<font color="#FFFFFF">raised to a power beta, where beta equals w plus one.</font>

35
00:02:26,876 --> 00:02:30,840
<font color="#FFFFFF">They take a Taylor expansion of the score predictor
in beta,</font>

36
00:02:30,920 --> 00:02:32,794
<font color="#FFFFFF">evaluated at beta equals one.</font>

37
00:02:32,874 --> 00:02:36,636
<font color="#FFFFFF">The first-order term is exactly classifier-free guidance.</font>

38
00:02:36,716 --> 00:02:42,703
<font color="#FFFFFF">The second-order term is new: it is estimated using
a middle point m between zero and one,</font>

39
00:02:42,783 --> 00:02:47,758
<font color="#FFFFFF">and needs only one extra score evaluation at a
scaled condition, m times c.</font>

40
00:02:47,838 --> 00:02:51,667
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because everything comes from the already-trained network,</font>

41
00:02:51,747 --> 00:02:57,329
<font color="#FFFFFF">second-order ICFG drops into a pretrained Stable
Diffusion with a few lines of code,</font>

42
00:02:57,409 --> 00:03:01,710
<font color="#FFFFFF">controlled by a first-order weight w and a
second-order weight v.</font>

43
00:03:02,090 --> 00:03:06,455
<font color="#FFFFFF">The main evaluation is text-to-image on the MS-COCO
validation set.</font>

44
00:03:06,535 --> 00:03:10,701
<font color="#FFFFFF">Two metrics carry the story: FID, which measures
image fidelity,</font>

45
00:03:10,781 --> 00:03:13,222
<font color="#FFFFFF">where lower is better, and CLIP Score,</font>

46
00:03:13,303 --> 00:03:18,331
<font color="#FFFFFF">which measures how well the image matches the
prompt, where higher is better.</font>

47
00:03:18,411 --> 00:03:21,848
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors run these on pretrained Stable Diffusion,</font>

48
00:03:21,928 --> 00:03:29,610
<font color="#FFFFFF">and add a class-conditional experiment on U-ViT
plus a few-shot fine-tuning study on Stable Diffusion one point five.</font>

49
00:03:29,990 --> 00:03:34,291
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline finding is that the second-order term
genuinely helps.</font>

50
00:03:34,371 --> 00:03:39,457
<font color="#FFFFFF">On MS-COCO, at guidance strength two with the
second-order weight at a quarter,</font>

51
00:03:39,537 --> 00:03:45,473
<font color="#FFFFFF">ICFG reaches an FID of fifteen point two eight and
a CLIP score of twenty six point one one.</font>

52
00:03:45,553 --> 00:03:49,135
<font color="#FFFFFF">That beats classifier-free guidance at the same setting,</font>

53
00:03:49,215 --> 00:03:55,217
<font color="#FFFFFF">with a worse FID of fifteen point four two and a
lower CLIP score of twenty five point eight.</font>

54
00:03:55,297 --> 00:03:59,075
<font color="#FFFFFF">So the method improves both fidelity and alignment
at once,</font>

55
00:03:59,155 --> 00:04:00,775
<font color="#FFFFFF">without touching training.</font>

56
00:04:00,855 --> 00:04:05,810
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors identify the sweet spot at w equals two
and v equals one quarter,</font>

57
00:04:05,890 --> 00:04:10,780
<font color="#FFFFFF">and note the full condition space trades off the
two metrics more favorably.</font>

58
00:04:11,160 --> 00:04:15,007
<font color="#FFFFFF">Two ablations illuminate the design choices.</font>

59
00:04:15,088 --> 00:04:18,935
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, the middle point m, used to estimate the
second-order term,</font>

60
00:04:19,015 --> 00:04:22,863
<font color="#FFFFFF">produces a U-shaped FID curve: if the two points
are too close,</font>

61
00:04:22,942 --> 00:04:26,790
<font color="#FFFFFF">the estimate can't capture long-term change, and if
they drift too near zero or one,</font>

62
00:04:26,870 --> 00:04:30,718
<font color="#FFFFFF">the model struggles to score them, with the best
value around m equals one point one.</font>

63
00:04:30,798 --> 00:04:34,645
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second,</font>

64
00:04:34,725 --> 00:04:38,573
<font color="#FFFFFF">varying the sampling steps shows the method already
produces well-matched images at just ten steps,</font>

65
00:04:38,653 --> 00:04:42,500
<font color="#FFFFFF">improving modestly up to fifty.</font>

66
00:04:42,880 --> 00:04:46,558
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers to take away.</font>

67
00:04:46,638 --> 00:04:50,316
<font color="#FFFFFF">The best fidelity-alignment balance on MS-COCO is
an FID of fifteen point two eight and</font>

68
00:04:50,396 --> 00:04:54,073
<font color="#FFFFFF">a CLIP score of twenty six point one one,</font>

69
00:04:54,153 --> 00:04:57,831
<font color="#FFFFFF">beating classifier-free guidance on both.</font>

70
00:04:57,911 --> 00:05:01,589
<font color="#FFFFFF">In the middle-point sweep,</font>

71
00:05:01,669 --> 00:05:05,347
<font color="#FFFFFF">the best FID is fifteen point four two at m equal
to one point one.</font>

72
00:05:05,427 --> 00:05:09,104
<font color="#FFFFFF">On U-ViT,</font>

73
00:05:09,184 --> 00:05:12,862
<font color="#FFFFFF">inner classifier-free guidance cuts FID
substantially at low sampling budgets and edges out CFG at high budgets.</font>

74
00:05:12,942 --> 00:05:16,620
<font color="#FFFFFF">And all of it comes from a few lines of code, with
no change to training.</font>

75
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:18,399
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway is simple.</font>

76
00:05:18,479 --> 00:05:23,222
<font color="#FFFFFF">Classifier-free guidance is just the first-order
slice of a richer picture.</font>

77
00:05:23,302 --> 00:05:27,723
<font color="#FFFFFF">Treat the guided score as a Taylor expansion in the
guidance strength,</font>

78
00:05:27,803 --> 00:05:33,125
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a single extra second-order term buys you a
better fidelity-diversity trade-off.</font>

79
00:05:33,205 --> 00:05:37,755
<font color="#FFFFFF">It needs no retraining, only a scaled condition and
a few lines of code,</font>

80
00:05:37,835 --> 00:05:43,800
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it points toward exploiting the continuous
condition structure that plain CFG throws away.</font>
